D-pad navigation for custom class creation (CreateClassDialog + SelectSpecializationDialog).

Two separate upstream gaps, both in apps/openmw/mwgui/class.cpp:

1. CreateClassDialog::onControllerButtonEvent only cycled mButtons - the three
   dialog buttons (Description / Back / OK) - on D-pad LEFT/RIGHT, and handled
   UP/DOWN not at all. mControllerFocus initialised to 2, so the focus started on
   OK and could never leave the button row. The thirteen fields the screen exists
   to edit (SpecializationName, FavoriteAttribute0/1, MajorSkill0..4,
   MinorSkill0..4) were in no focus set, making custom class creation unusable
   without the stick cursor. Note the dialog deliberately does not set
   mDisableGamepadCursor and advertises mLStick = Mouse, i.e. upstream expected the
   cursor to cover these fields; OpenMW-DS ships the "Game cursor" option off by
   default (companion-gamecursor-suppress.patch), so nothing covered them.

   Replaced with a navigation grid matching the layout, which is already three
   columns over a button row (openmw_chargen_create_class.layout):

       EditName  (spans the full width, leads every column)
       col 0            col 1 (x=166)   col 2 (x=332)
       SpecializationName  MajorSkill0     MinorSkill0
       FavoriteAttribute0  MajorSkill1     MinorSkill1
       FavoriteAttribute1  MajorSkill2     MinorSkill2
                           MajorSkill3     MinorSkill3
                           MajorSkill4     MinorSkill4
       [Description]       [Back]          [OK]

   Each column owns the button beneath it, so a column is [fields..., button] and
   the button is always the last entry. The class-name edit box spans the whole
   width above the columns, so it is the FIRST entry of every column - Up from the
   top field reaches it from any column, and moving sideways along that row lands
   on the same widget again. UP/DOWN wrap within a column; LEFT/RIGHT
   change column, keeping the button row when already on a button and otherwise
   clamping into the new column's field range (so leaving MajorSkill4 for the
   three-entry first column cannot land on Description). A activates whatever is
   focused, opening the same picker a mouse click opens. B (Back) and X (OK) are
   unchanged.

2. SelectSpecializationDialog::onControllerButtonEvent handled B only, while its
   own hint bar advertised mA = Select - so the specialization picker could be
   opened but never used with a controller. Given A and D-pad UP/DOWN in the same
   shape as its siblings SelectAttributeDialog and SelectSkillDialog, which were
   already complete and are untouched here.

Implementation notes:

- Uses the existing per-window pattern (a focus index plus wrap() and
  setControllerFocus() from windowbase.cpp). There is no general MyGUI focus-graph
  system to opt into.
- Highlighting needs three unrelated setStateSelected() overloads that share no
  base class: MWSkill and MWAttribute recolour their name label (widgets.cpp),
  while MyGUI::Button uses the skin's "*_checked" states. highlightNavItem()
  picks by castType rather than by the stored kind, so a widget can never be
  highlighted through the wrong overload.
- The specialization rows are declared MyGUI::TextBox* in class.hpp but are
  type="Button" in openmw_chargen_select_specialization.layout, so they are
  re-fetched as buttons for highlighting only; activation still goes through the
  TextBox pointers that onSpecializationClicked() compares against.
- The D-pad hint uses the existing "#{Interface:Move}" key (already the D-pad hint
  on the map window) rather than adding a new l10n string that would be
  untranslated in the five non-English locales.
- The class-name field is a MyGUI::EditBox, which has no setStateSelected(), so it
  is highlighted by recolouring its text exactly as MWSkill/MWAttribute do. It is
  tested BEFORE the Button branch: EditBox and Button both derive from TextBox, but
  an EditBox is not a Button, so the Button branch would miss it.
- Activating the name field calls setKeyFocusWidget(mEditName) - the same thing a
  click does, and what raises the on-screen keyboard via
  companion-text-input-export.patch's onKeyFocusChanged hook. Key focus is only ever
  moved TO a widget, never cleared: that patch's own notes record that OpenMW's modal
  text dialogs re-grab a cleared field on the very next frame, so clearing focus when
  navigating away would churn the keyboard open and shut rather than dismiss it.
- buildControllerNav() runs unconditionally so the grid is never empty; only the
  initial highlight and the hint bar are gated on Settings::gui().mControllerMenus.
- Focus still starts on OK, as before, so pressing A immediately still accepts the
  default class.

